{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjXoJjseeVRvnipvaHcc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Wee Teeter is finished.** The full test command now passes on the exact file I'm handing in: 1730 passed, 0 failed. The file's checksum is the same before and after that run, and I made no code or build changes afterwards.\n\n- **`STAGE.md`:** it has no further step. It asks for three things: build the game from `PLAN.md`, take file names and hand-ins from `TASK.md`, and keep progress in `PROGRESS.md`. All three are done.\n- **Hand-in:** the 36 hand-in checks all pass on that file. The HTML, `exit-interview.json` and dev folder use the names `TASK.md` asks for and sit in `work`, the folder with `STAGE.md`, and the interview parses.\n- **`PLAN.md` section 18:** `PROGRESS.md` lists what backs each line.\n\n**What no script can settle:**\n- **Tone and originality:** the tests prove the plan's lines are word for word, and that activity and cast names come from the plan with no source names in game text. Whether my other lines and my 14 songs are good and original is my judgment.\n- **Feel on a real thumb:** nobody has played it by hand. A modelled person earns stars on every activity through the keyboard, mouse and stick paths, which shows it's playable, not that it's fun.\n- **Hardware:** a real controller, touch device, iPhone, TV and second player were tested through the stand-ins `TASK.md` prescribes, and it says the real things are never required.\n\n**Where the game differs from the plan's start numbers:**\n- **Laundry Line:** keyboard walking is on W and mouse jumping on the right button, because Space was both Effort and Jump. Keys move faster on the rope and it's kinder to them, its scoring runs par 56 s to worst 220 s, and its tier 2 is a little gentler.\n- **Rail exercises:** they respond faster (k 26, c 10).\n- **Tray Service:** it runs 70 s, and 90 s on tier 2.\n\nI removed `node_modules`, so run `npm install` in the dev folder before re-running the browser tests. Nothing is committed to git.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 22:10:37\n- **Source:** Wii Fit\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre:** Sports and fitness minigame collection inside a light life-sim routine\n- **Perspective:** Per activity: 2.5D fixed (brawler view) in the studios, three-quarter on the island, Mode 7 behind the skier, pseudo-3D road behind the runner, a steep view of the tray, side view on the laundry line\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge full engine v0.89.0 (Mode 7, road, physics and rhythm modules, Mocap with a cut clip set), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript in a single HTML file\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native look: 3D skeleton puppets drawn as crisp pixel art at ps1 resolution, moved into bright daylight. New art in code: Tare, Gerald, wooden mannequin joints, the Plate, rooms, tray, laundry, gates\n- **Animation:** The engine's Humanoid rigs driven by motion capture (Mesh2Motion, Quaternius, CMU), with a hand-posed body layer (side lunge, hula, step-up, rope walk) and a wobble layer on top\n- **Audio:** All written in code with no files: 14 original songs and 5 jingles in step notation with music at 10% by default, about 30 synthesized sound effects, and Animalese-style gibberish voices made letter by letter through a band-pass filter\n- **Online:** AI Hub session in ghost mode (1 to 4 players, the Morning Class round) and local Class for 2 to 4 on one screen; no other network\n- **Testing:** As the brief allowed: headless scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game, behind one command that prints only failures and a count; a modelled person on each device path; one-device walkthroughs of a whole first day with a stand-in pad, real keys and a real pointer; touch profiles and audio probes; the AI Hub harness; screenshots only for what a script cannot judge; no subagents or workflows\n- **Research:** None beyond the plan, the engine's documents and the hub protocol README\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 35. 16 came from the scripted tests: for example the attract timer that never fired, a mouse that could not lean, a results screen that swallowed the story's next press, a jog list that skipped its water cups, tray gaps that left no way through, a rope bot that stopped jumping a peg after one trip, and Laundry Line on keys, which a modelled person could not finish. The rest came from screenshots: overlapping HUD, a towel across the face, poses that turned when bent over.\n- **How many would be considered critical or game breaking?** 4: attract mode never started, the mouse could not lean for player 1, the story could stall on the results screen, and Laundry Line could not be finished on a keyboard.\n- **Most helpful test:** The scripted playthrough of the whole game with real taps and bots, run once with a good bot and once with a passive player. A close second: a modelled person playing every activity through the real keyboard, mouse and stick paths, which found the keyboard rope.\n- **Least helpful test:** The Firefox and WebKit smoke tests. They passed the first time and have not caught anything yet, though they cost little.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The exact fixed step and the headless draw: a whole five-day story plays in Node in minutes with the same results every time. After that, how much is already there: rigs with real motion capture, Mode 7, the road, rhythm, physics, and pause, settings and rebinding I did not have to write.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** Three wrong guesses. `game.hook` passes only dt, not (game, dt), which made an idle timer NaN. `input.player` is undefined until `game.players` has been read. And `rig.spin` is not trustworthy for a bent-over clip, so I read one heading from the hips for held poses.\n- **Most important thing built:** The Lean: one balance model with an intent layer that makes a stick, keys and a mouse each native, then the activity runtime on top that hosts a run in a session, Class, attract, the Games and a hub round.\n- **Hardest part:** Reading a rig correctly in several views and facings at low resolution, and keeping a 10-foot HUD legible with a 5x7 font at 427 x 240. `E.Rhythm` also reads `game.input` directly, so per-player Class needed a proxy game object.\n- **Changed in the engine:** The Dialog's hard-coded dark text shadow now follows `talk.shadow` in 3 places, and its box shadows follow it too, because the shadows smeared on a white box. Nothing else.\n- **One change:** A stable heading for bent-over poses in `Mocap.drive`, read from the hips, so a held Down Dog does not turn when its chest has no heading to trust.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The plan's tested recipes and verbatim lines. The tone was already there, and the numbers marked as tested were right: the balance table reproduced almost exactly on the first run.\n- **Confusing:** Space is both Effort and Jump on the Laundry Line, so I moved keyboard walking to W and mouse jumping to the right button. The plan says keys at 2.2/s score the same as a stick, but on the rope my modelled person fell constantly, so keys there move faster and the rope is a little kinder to them. The file name also does not say how to write a SOURCE with a space, so I used WiiFit. The plan was tested on engine v0.88.0 and the handoff is v0.89.0, which caused no trouble.\n- **Hardest thing:** Tuning twelve activities without being able to feel them. Bots can hold a star band, but only judgment says whether a thumb will enjoy it.\n- **Would change:** Resolve the Space conflict in the input table, recheck the keyboard claim on the rope, say how to write a SOURCE with spaces in the file name, and add a short list of the feelings to check by hand for whoever can play it.\n\nFiles are in `/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-221037__x61bd7e/work`:\n- `WeeTeeter-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-221037.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `WeeTeeter-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-221037/`\n- `PROGRESS.md`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1227,"cache_read_input_tokens":904995,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1227},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e35d4c2a-4185-48d4-8818-8a6b8bcd7047","uuid":"49b21607-8ecc-4f05-896c-eb28af20aff6","timestamp":"2026-11-05T09:19:08.838Z","request_id":"req_011CgjXoHbvC3UJJpmNMtJdC"}
